on (`assert yaml.__version__ == \"6.0.3\"; assert jsonschema.__version__ == \"4.26.0\"; assert fastapi.__version__ == \"0.136.1\"; assert pydantic.VERSION == \"2.13.3\"`) defending against future-PR drift (mirrors 020M SAFE-MAJ-002 pattern). Deliberate pyyaml-version drift accepted: conflict-gate.txt resolves pyyaml==6.0.3, policy-check.txt pins pyyaml==6.0.2 — independent workflows with independent supply-chain anchors; both hash-anchored. **No semver bump.** Per `policies/VERSIONING.md` §\"Scope\", `conflict-gate.yml` is not in the public-surface enumeration (workflow_call inputs on `policy-check.yml`, schema contracts, rule IDs); it is a SCP-self-only workflow that adopters do not consume. Therefore no `policies/deprecations.yaml` entry, no version-manifest bump, no release notes — internal hardening | ACCEPTED | Symmetric posture with D-038 (slice 020M hash-pinning of policy-check.yml + release-gate.yml). The conflict-gate is the third Python-installing workflow in the SCP repo; closing the unhardened install completes the Python supply-chain hardening across all three SCP-self workflows that pip-install at runtime. Threat surface (PyPI compromise of conflict-gate's deps) was acknowledged at 020M R1 safety as MIN-class because conflict-gate is SCP-self-only (not adopter-facing); a compromise would degrade SCP self's policy-evaluator quality but not directly compromise adopters. The user's directive to clear all outstanding TFs (rather than defer to v1.1 maintenance) raised this from forward-compat to in-flight closure. The `--no-deps` editable install of the local SCP package is the tradeoff: pip refuses `--require-hashes` on editable installs, so the local source tree is installed without re-resolving (its PyPI deps are already hash-verified above). The `ct-auth` vendored wheel is similarly outside `--require-hashes` (pip refuses for local file: URLs); chain-of-trust is the `vendor/**` CODEOWNERS coverage + the wheel being in-tree (no network fetch). Identified at 020M R1 safety SAFE-MIN-003 closure |\n| D-040 | 2026-05-02 | Amend the rule-RFC process (`docs/reviews/rule-proposals/README.md` §Process step 2) so that the 48h wall-clock review window is a CEILING (auto-defer trigger) rather than a FLOOR (minimum wait time) **in single-operator mode** (D-031 default at v1.0.0). Rationale: in single-operator mode the proposal author IS the only `SCP-CODEOWNERS` member; there are no external reviewers for the wall-clock pause to enable. The recursive 3-lens R1/R2 adversarial review (per `feedback_recursive_adversarial_review.md`) IS the adversarial-review surface; the wall-clock pause was vestigial theatre that produced no additional safety. The operator MAY merge a proposal as soon as adversarial review reaches fixpoint (no new CRIT/MAJ on a complete cycle) AND the proposal PR is open with the `scp-rule-proposal` label applied. The 48h CEILING remains in force: zero approvals by 48h still triggers auto-defer (`defer` label per slice 020L label-provisioning). When a second maintainer onboards (D-031 escalation, 2026-07-21 review), the 48h becomes a mandatory MINIMUM to give the second maintainer time to review — the CEILING-not-FLOOR posture is single-operator-only. Bypass-introducing proposals (`Bypass-surface non-empty: true`) retain their non-waivable 48h MINIMUM regardless of operator-count posture, because the additional adversarial-review value of a wall-clock pause IS material when a new bypass surface is being introduced (the operator may catch their own bypass-introducing PR's flaws on second look more reliably than for a non-bypass additive rule). Surfaced by 020L dogfood (the first RFC actually walked through the process). Closes WP-SCP-022 020L post-R2 process-gap | ACCEPTED | The 020L dogfood surfaced this gap operationally: the operator (single-maintainer) reached R1+R2 fixpoint on a non-bypass proposal and was about to wait 48h before merging — at which point the wait was identified as theatre rather than safety (no second pair of eyes to chime in). Theatre-vs-safety is the doctrine question the dogfood is designed to surface cheaply. The amendment narrows the discretionary posture to single-operator + non-bypass + post-fixpoint, leaving every other shape of the 48h window unchanged: bypass-introducing proposals keep the non-waivable minimum (the threat-surface justifies the wait); the post-2nd-maintainer-onboard 48h becomes mandatory minimum so the second maintainer has time to review; the 48h CEILING for auto-defer remains so abandoned proposals are surfaced. Symmetric with D-033's \"operational reality must amend the spec when the spec encounters a constraint that wasn't anticipated\" pattern. Identified at WP-SCP-022 slice 020L post-R2 fixpoint by operator (`@jrnb2024`) when reviewing the merge-readiness of PR #89 |\n| D-041 | 2026-05-03 | Adopt the cross-repo scorecard data shape (`schemas/scorecard-emit.schema.json` v0.1) + opt-in adopter participation model per WP-SCP-023 023B (this PR). The schema defines aggregated per-rule counts (raw_findings, denies, waived, rule_config_disabled), waivers aggregate (active_count, by_rule_id, expiring_within_30d), rule-config aggregate (disabled_rules, expiring_within_30d), repo / ref / commit / scp_version / RFC3339 emitted_at, and a verdict (allow/deny/warn). The schema EXPLICITLY DOES NOT include waiver content (`reason`, `approved_by`, `waiver_id` strings) — invariant 2 of WP-SCP-023 plan-doc binds this. The reusable workflow gains a `scorecard-emit: bool` `workflow_call` input (default false) so existing adopters see no behaviour change; opt-in adopters set `with: scorecard-emit: true`. Schema versioning: MAJOR-pinned per `policies/VERSIONING.md` D-036 + WP-SCP-023 plan-doc §10 Q1 — aggregator drops emits with unknown `schema_version`. Verification: GitHub Actions Artifact Attestation (OIDC-based) via `actions/attest-build-provenance@v4.1.0` (SHA `a2bbfa25...`) at emit time; aggregator verifies via `gh attestation verify --signer-workflow` (per D-042 to be filed at slice 023C). v1.2.0 cut on this slice's merge per VERSIONING.md MINOR category | ACCEPTED | This is the first slice of WP-SCP-023's implementation chain (023A plan-doc landed at PR #97, commit `7075234`, 2026-05-03 R4 fixpoint). The data shape was specified at plan-doc level; this slice ratifies it as code surface. Default-false on the workflow input is the load-bearing safety property: existing adopters cannot have new behaviour spring on them; opt-in is explicit per invariant 4. The waiver-content exclusion (invariant 2) is enforced by the schema's `additionalProperties: false` constraint on every level + the explicit field enumeration. The MAJOR-pinning posture means schema breakage requires v2.0.0 + adopter migration; v0.1 prefix on `schema_version` reflects the freedom to amend until first adopter opts in. Bus-factor-1 acknowledged: aggregator runs on the SCP repo with single-operator identity (D-031); 2026-07-21 quarterly review covers. Identified at WP-SCP-023 023A plan-doc §9 D-041 reservation |\n| D-042 | 2026-05-03 | Adopt the cross-repo scorecard aggregator pipeline trust model per WP-SCP-023 023C (this PR). Mechanism: per-emit verification via `gh attestation verify --signer-workflow <expected_workflow_ref>` (the GitHub Actions Artifact Attestation OIDC pattern, mandatory per the D-042 reservation note above + 023A R2 MAJ-SAFE-R2-001 closure); index trust rooted in git's required-signed-commits (slice 020J) + `output/scorecards/** @jrnb2024` CODEOWNERS coverage (slice 023A) + branch protection on main (slice 020D2), NOT a separate Ed25519 key layer. The aggregator runs as `.github/workflows/scorecard-aggregator.yml` on a weekly cron (Monday 06:00 UTC) + `workflow_dispatch`; two-job structure (aggregate read-only + commit write-scoped via job-level permissions, mirroring the 023B `attest-scorecard` job split); reads `docs/scorecards/opt-in-registry.yaml` (CODEOWNERS-protected at `docs/scorecards/**`), pulls each adopter's latest green policy-check run's `scorecard-emit.json` artifact, verifies attestation, schema-validates emit, aggregates into `output/scorecards/index.json` (schema: `schemas/scorecard-index.schema.json`), and opens a PR for operator review. Failure modes (verification_failure / unreachable / no_emit) record loudly per-adopter — never silently dropped. NEVER carries waiver content (invariant 2 enforced by schema `additionalProperties: false` at every level). Internal-only workflow per `policies/VERSIONING.md` §\"Scope\" (mirrors 020N conflict-gate.yml posture); no semver bump. Verifies WP-SCP-023 plan-doc invariant 6 (\"Aggregator output is Ed25519-signed\") in spirit — Sigstore/Fulcio uses Ed25519 under the hood, managed by GitHub rather than by an SCP-side private key, eliminating the private-key-distribution surface | ACCEPTED | Symmetric posture with D-041 (per-emit data shape + opt-in model) — D-041 ratified the EMITTER side of the trust model; D-042 ratifies the AGGREGATOR / VERIFIER side. The mandatory `--signer-workflow` constraint was already pre-committed at the D-042 reservation note above (closes 023A R2 MAJ-SAFE-R2-001) — this slice ratifies it as workflow code. The Ed25519-via-Sigstore framing eliminates the chicken-and-egg of \"where does the SCP private key live and how does the aggregator hold it without exposing adopters\" — GitHub manages the keys via OIDC + Sigstore. The two-job permission split (aggregate read-only + commit write-scoped) is identical to 023B's `policy-check` + `attest-scorecard` split (closes the analogous threat surface where workflow-level write permissions could be exploited by a compromised aggregate-side step). Bus-factor-1 acknowledged: aggregator runs on the SCP repo with single-operator identity (D-031); 2026-07-21 quarterly review covers. Identified at WP-SCP-023 023A plan-doc §9 D-042 reservation + 023A R2 MAJ-SAFE-R2-001 closure |\n| D-044 | 2026-05-09 | Adopt the WP-SCP-024 slice 024B operational contracts for cascade scaffolding (this PR). Components: (1) `scripts/scaffold-downstream.sh` (= `SCP-073-scaffolder` per WP-SCP-020 §11 follow-up + WP-SCP-021 follow-up `SCP-075-scaffolder-compose`) generates adopter-side artefacts mechanically — bootstrap-only (refuses `CI=true` / `GITHUB_ACTIONS=true` per D-035 symmetry); validates `--scp-sha` against SCP main HEAD lineage (warns if not main HEAD; refuses if non-existent); emits `MANIFEST.json` with SHA256 audit; arguments `--adopter-repo`, `--default-branch`, `--scp-sha`, `--scorecard-emit`, `--output-dir` per plan-doc §5.3. (2) `templates/adopter-wrapper.yml.tmpl` is the canonical adopter wrapper shape per ADOPT-001 §12; future wrapper-shape changes land in the template + propagate via Renovate SHA bumps. (3) `scripts/enable-required-check.sh --restore <pre-state.json>` mode reverses prior branch-protection mutation from a captured before-state and now validates that captured before-state as a legitimate GitHub branch-protection payload (required_status_checks object, enforce_admins.enabled boolean, required_pull_request_reviews object-or-null, restrictions object-or-null). Restore mode captures the actually-observed current branch-protection state immediately before PUT, then logs the observed degraded state separately so the audit trail shows both the target good-state and the broken pre-restore state. New safety check (forward mode only): resolves the `policy-check` workflow id, then refuses to invoke unless the repo has at least one successful `policy-check` workflow run in the last 60 days; if no `policy-check` workflow is registered, or no successful runs exist in the 60-day window, it exits with the existing cold-start error unless `--i-understand-this-repo-has-no-prior-green-ci` is set. (4) `scripts/check-invocation-log-entry.sh` CI enforces invariant 2's 4 cascade-status check behaviours: fail-closed default for absent/unrecognised `cascade-status:`; `onboarded` requires log entry + target match; `onboarded-operator-bump` additionally requires STATUS.md `TF-024X-renovate-<adopter-slug>` row matching regex literally; `blocked-on-adopter-conflict` requires DISPATCH-NOTE `TF-024X-conflict-<adopter>` reference matching regex literally + log file NOT modified. Regex (Python re): `TF-024X-(renovate|conflict)-[a-z0-9]+(?:-[a-z0-9]+)+(?:\\*\\*)? \\((open|pending|in-progress|closed)\\): \\S.{19,}` — matches in STATUS.md or DISPATCH-NOTE; `\\S` anchor at description start prevents all-whitespace bypass; ≥20 chars total. (5) ADOPT-001 §12.8 break-glass procedure — 3-gate playbook (disable via `--restore` → SHA-pin to last known-good release tag → re-enable after Renovate-bumped fix). All four script components (scaffolder + `--restore` + `--i-understand` flag + `check-invocation-log-entry.sh` covering all four CI behaviours) are hard predecessors for slice 024C. Slice 024B itself ships under documented protocol deviation (direct codex exec / claude -p; non-kernel-dangerous shell + tests + docs scope) per `feedback_four_tier_dispatch.md` note + justify rule; 024C entry is conditional on `FUP-ACC-INSTALL-TARGET-REPO-001` closure (per-repo dispatcher install pattern resolved in ACC). | ACCEPTED | D-044 was reserved at WP-SCP-024 plan-doc 024A merge (PR #102, 2026-05-04) per the §9 reservation block; this slice ratifies it. The four-component scope (scaffolder + template + `--restore` + `check-invocation-log-entry.sh`) is the single hardest predecessor surface for cohort cascade — without all four, cascade slices 024C-F would each rebuild the per-adopter wrapper shape, the rollback procedure, and the cascade-status invariant enforcement from scratch (multiplying the 9-round adversarial-review burden that 024A absorbed by 4 cohort slices). The `MANIFEST.json` + SHA256 audit surface mirrors the `install_acc_hook.sh` pattern (atomic config + binary SHA pinning) — provides forensic traceability for cascade-driven adopter-side artefact authorship. The `--i-understand-this-repo-has-no-prior-green-ci` flag closes the cold-start chicken-and-egg: a new adopter has no green CI before the wrapper merges + at least one PR runs, so the safety check would otherwise block first-onboard; the explicit flag is the operator's acknowledgement that they accept the cold-start risk for that single first-onboard run. The break-glass 3-gate procedure is intentionally NOT a one-button kill-switch (all gates auditable in git history; Gate 2's wrapper-SHA-pin is explicit operator consent to step backwards from current SCP main; Gate 3 ensures adopters don't stay un-gated forever). Bus-factor-1 acknowledged (D-031); 2026-07-21 quarterly review covers. Identified at WP-SCP-024 plan-doc §9 D-044 reservation. |\n\n====\n# Standards Control Plane — STATUS\n\n**Last updated:** 2026-05-09 (WP-SCP-024 024B - scaffolder + --restore + invocation-log CI; D-044 filed; previous: 2026-05-09 D-035-rules retired)\n\n## At-a-glance\n\n| Programme | State | Track |\n|---|---|---|\n| WP-SCP-019 (Service Auth Contract) | ✅ closed 2026-04-20 | — |\n| WP-SCP-020 (Policy Federation Primitive) | ✅ **closed 2026-04-30** at v1.0.0 (Threshold A) | Track 1 |\n| WP-SCP-021 (MCP Server) | ✅ landed 2026-04-29 (USER-GATE-C signed) | Track 2 |\n| WP-SCP-022 (Implementation Programme) | ✅ **closed 2026-04-30** (USER-GATE-A signed) | Meta |\n\n## Threshold A — REACHED 2026-04-30\n\n> **Threshold A definition:** SCP gates itself on its own `main` via the federation primitive's reusable workflow with a required-status-check, all three v1.0.0 Rego rules enforcing, conflict-gate green on every PR, and a v1.0.0 release tag cut.\n\n```\n020A    plan + D-022 + D-023                                     ✅ landed\n020B    reusable workflow                                        ✅ landed (PR #36)\n020B.1  workflow-selftest harness                                ✅ landed (PR #38)\n020B.2  scripts/scp-policy-check local repro                     ✅ landed (PR #41)\n020C    starter Rego rule library (3 rules)                 