{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json,re,pathlib\nfor p in [\n 'docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/024B/r1-correctness.json',\n 'docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/024B/r1-safety_bypass.json',\n 'docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/024B/r1-completeness_governance.json']:\n    text=pathlib.Path(p).read_text()\n    m=re.search(r'\\\\{.*\\\\}', text, re.S)\n    print('FILE',p)\n    obj=json.loads(m.group(0))\n    print(obj.keys())\n    print(obj)\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json,re,pathlib\nfor p in [\n 'docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/024B/r1-correctness.json',\n 'docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/024B/r1-safety_bypass.json',\n 'docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/024B/r1-completeness_governance.json']:\n    text=pathlib.Path(p).read_text()\n    m=re.search(r'\\\\{.*\\\\}', text, re.S)\n    print('FILE',p)\n    obj=json.loads(m.group(0))\n    print(obj.keys())\n    print(obj)\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"FILE docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/024B/r1-correctness.json\ndict_keys(['type', 'subtype', 'is_error', 'api_error_status', 'duration_ms', 'duration_api_ms', 'num_turns', 'result', 'stop_reason', 'session_id', 'total_cost_usd', 'usage', 'modelUsage', 'permission_denials', 'terminal_reason', 'fast_mode_state', 'uuid'])\n{'type': 'result', 'subtype': 'success', 'is_error': False, 'api_error_status': None, 'duration_ms': 961859, 'duration_api_ms': 962424, 'num_turns': 20, 'result': 'I now have all the information needed to compile a complete correctness review. Let me produce the final JSON result.\\n\\n{\"round\":\"R1\",\"lens\":\"correctness\",\"slice\":\"WP-SCP-024-024B\",\"work_package\":\"WP-SCP-024\",\"verdict\":\"BLOCKED\",\"new_crit_count\":1,\"new_maj_count\":2,\"new_min_count\":2,\"new_nit_count\":2,\"findings\":[{\"id\":\"R1-COR-CRIT-001\",\"severity\":\"CRIT\",\"claim\":\"templates/adopter-wrapper.yml.tmpl is missing the Renovate inline-comment marker present on the canonical SCP self-dogfood wrapper, making it impossible for Renovate to auto-detect and bump the SHA pin in generated adopter wrappers.\",\"evidence\":\"The canonical SCP self-dogfood wrapper at .github/workflows/policy-check-wrapper.yml line 56 carries `# renovate: datasource=github-tags depName=jrnb2024/standards-control-plane-` immediately before the `uses:` line. The template at templates/adopter-wrapper.yml.tmpl lines 16-17 has only `# Pin must be a 40-char SHA per WP-SCP-020 020B(v).` followed by the `uses:` line — no renovate datasource comment. A Glob for `renovate/**` in the current working tree returns no files, so no `customManagers` override in a local renovate preset can be confirmed to provide an alternative detection mechanism. The renovate.json extends `github>jrnb2024/standards-control-plane-//renovate/default#renovate/v1.0.0` (a tagged version not present in this branch\\'s tree), and without reading that file the absence of comment-free SHA detection cannot be ruled in.\",\"impact\":\"Without the `# renovate:` comment, Renovate cannot identify the SHA pin in any adopter wrapper generated from this template and cannot issue automatic SHA-bump PRs to adopters. Every cascade adopter is structurally forced into the `onboarded-operator-bump` close-state (operator-manually bumps SHA), which (a) requires filing a `TF-024X-renovate-<slug>` tracked-forward entry for every adopter, (b) disqualifies those adopters from counting toward Threshold A until a separate real Renovate cycle subsequently runs per plan-doc §8 (\\'Adopters who satisfied the per-slice bake via an operator-driven bump do NOT count toward Threshold A until a real Renovate cycle has also merged + observed clean\\'), and (c) makes invariant 8 (bake EXTENDS until at least one Renovate-issued SHA pin bump has merged + observed clean) unachievable through the scaffolder\\'s own output alone. The entire `onboarded` close-state path and the Threshold A Renovate-cycle criterion are rendered unreachable for all 5 cohort adopters by a single missing comment line in the template.\",\"mitigation\":\"Add `# renovate: datasource=github-tags depName=jrnb2024/standards-control-plane-` to templates/adopter-wrapper.yml.tmpl immediately before the `uses:` line (matching the position in the canonical wrapper). The substituted output would then carry the marker, enabling Renovate auto-bumps on adopter repos that have Renovate installed. If the renovate/v1.0.0 preset already contains a `customManagers` regex entry that detects the SHA pattern in `policy-check-wrapper.yml` files without needing the inline comment, add a header comment to the template documenting this and cross-referencing the preset configuration so the relationship is explicit and auditable.\"},{\"id\":\"R1-COR-MAJ-001\",\"severity\":\"MAJ\",\"claim\":\"The --restore mode in scripts/enable-required-check.sh sends the GitHub GET-response format `enforce_admins` object (`{\\\\\"url\\\\\":\\\\\"...\\\\\",\\\\\"enabled\\\\\":true}`) directly to the branch-protection PUT endpoint, which documents the field as a plain boolean. This is a request-body contract violation that could cause the PUT to fail with HTTP 422 and break the <30-min rollback SLO.\",\"evidence\":\"enable-required-check.sh line 379-381: `printf \\'%s\\' \\\\\"$restore_before_json\\\\\" | gh api -X PUT \\\\\"repos/${restore_repo}/branches/${restore_branch}/protection\\\\\" -H \\\\\"Accept: application/vnd.github+json\\\\\" --input - >/dev/null`. The `restore_before_json` is the serialised `before` object from the pre-state JSON (line 357). The Python validation at lines 339-340 confirms the script EXPECTS this field to be an object: `if not isinstance(enforce_admins, dict) or not isinstance(enforce_admins.get(\\'enabled\\'), bool)`. By contrast, the forward-mode `build_payload` at lines 450-471 uses `jq -n --argjson admins \\\\\"$ENFORCE_ADMINS\\\\\"` to supply `enforce_admins` as a plain boolean. The test suite\\'s fake `gh` in run_restore_case (test file lines 539-550) does not validate the PUT body content, so the type mismatch cannot be caught at test time. The GitHub branch-protection PUT endpoint documents `enforce_admins` as `boolean` in its OpenAPI schema.\",\"impact\":\"If the GitHub API rejects a PUT body where `enforce_admins` is an object rather than a boolean, the restore call exits non-zero and `set -euo pipefail` causes the script to terminate before emitting the markdown log block. The rollback is silent-fail: branch protection is not restored, no log entry is emitted, and the operator has no audit trail of the failed attempt. The <30-min SLO declared in plan-doc invariant 7 and ADOPT-001 §12.8 Gate 1 is broken precisely in the scenario where it matters most (federation-primitive defect driving urgent rollback). The real-repo round-trip evidence (restore-roundtrip-evidence.md) is not yet committed, so this defect may not have been exercised against the live API.\",\"mitigation\":\"Transform the captured `before` JSON into the PUT-compatible format before issuing the restore call. Extract the boolean value from `enforce_admins.enabled`, strip the `url` sub-field, and similarly remove the `url` and `checks` sub-fields from `required_status_checks` to match the PUT schema. A jq one-liner after the Python validation can perform the transformation: `restore_put_json=$(printf \\'%s\\' \\\\\"$restore_before_json\\\\\" | jq \\'(.enforce_admins) |= .enabled | if .required_status_checks != null then (.required_status_checks) |= del(.url, .checks) else . end\\')` and use `restore_put_json` instead of `restore_before_json` for the PUT call.\"},{\"id\":\"R1-COR-MAJ-002\",\"severity\":\"MAJ\",\"claim\":\"The test suite for check-invocation-log-entry.sh has no negative test case for `cascade-status: onboarded-operator-bump` with an unmodified branch-protection log. All five operator-bump test cases (4-8) have the log modified; the first guard (`branch_log_modified == 1`) is never exercised in the failing direction for this cascade-status value.\",\"evidence\":\"tests/check_invocation_log/test_check_invocation_log_entry.sh cases 4-8 (lines 249-360): case 4 is the happy path with a modified log; cases 5-8 each write to `docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch-protection-log.md` before committing, so `branch_log_modified=1` in all five. check-invocation-log-entry.sh line 182: `[ \\\\\"$branch_log_modified\\\\\" -eq 1 ] || die \\\\\"ERROR: branch-protection-log.md was not modified for cascade-status=onboarded-operator-bump\\\\\"` is the first guard in the `onboarded-operator-bump` path. No test exercises the path where this guard fires. For comparison, the analogous negative test for `onboarded` exists (case 2, lines 218-225) and the analogous negative test for `blocked-on-adopter-conflict` (log IS modified when it should not be) exists as case 10 (lines 372-391).\",\"impact\":\"A future refactor that accidentally removes or inverts the `branch_log_modified` guard for the `onboarded-operator-bump` path would pass all existing tests. An operator could file `cascade-status: onboarded-operator-bump` with a valid STATUS.md TF row but without actually running `enable-required-check.sh`, and CI would pass. This undermines the audit-trail invariant (plan-doc invariant 2: \\'Required-check enablement is operator-run + invocation-logged + CI-enforced\\') for the operator-bump path, which is already the higher-risk path (Renovate is disabled; operator is the sole actor).\",\"mitigation\":\"Add a test case: init_case_repo, write DISPATCH-NOTE with `cascade-status: onboarded-operator-bump` and a valid `- **Target:**` line, write STATUS.md with a regex-passing TF row, do NOT modify branch-protection-log.md (leave it empty as committed by init_case_repo), commit, run_case expecting exit 1 with stderr containing \\'ERROR: branch-protection-log.md was not modified for cascade-status=onboarded-operator-bump\\'.\"},{\"id\":\"R1-COR-MIN-001\",\"severity\":\"MIN\",\"claim\":\"The DISPATCH-NOTE scope table documents substitution placeholder names in lowercase (`{{default-branch}}`, `{{scp-sha}}`, `{{scorecard-emit}}`), but the template and sed invocations use UPPERCASE (`{{DEFAULT_BRANCH}}`, `{{SCP_SHA}}`, `{{SCORECARD_EMIT}}`). The implementation is internally consistent; the DISPATCH-NOTE documentation is incorrect.\",\"evidence\":\"DISPATCH-NOTE line 31: `Substitutions: {{default-branch}}, {{scp-sha}}, {{scorecard-emit}}`. templates/adopter-wrapper.yml.tmpl lines 6, 17, 19: `{{DEFAULT_BRANCH}}`, `{{SCP_SHA}}`, `{{SCORECARD_EMIT}}`. scripts/scaffold-downstream.sh lines 157-161: sed expressions `s/{{DEFAULT_BRANCH}}/.../g`, `s/{{SCP_SHA}}/.../g`, `s/{{SCORECARD_EMIT}}/.../g` all use uppercase.\",\"impact\":\"An operator or cascade-slice author who consults the DISPATCH-NOTE to understand the substitution contract (e.g., when manually patching a generated wrapper or verifying template output) would use incorrect placeholder names. Could mislead downstream documentation for 024C-F slice authors who copy the DISPATCH-NOTE pattern.\",\"mitigation\":\"Correct the DISPATCH-NOTE scope table entry for the adopter wrapper template row to use UPPERCASE placeholder names: `{{DEFAULT_BRANCH}}`, `{{SCP_SHA}}`, `{{SCORECARD_EMIT}}`.\"},{\"id\":\"R1-COR-MIN-002\",\"severity\":\"MIN\",\"claim\":\"The forward-mode prior-green-CI safety check in enable-required-check.sh queries workflow runs without filtering by branch, allowing a successful run on any PR branch to satisfy a check that is described as requiring green CI \\'on the default branch\\' in plan-doc §5.4.\",\"evidence\":\"scripts/enable-required-check.sh lines 261-269: `gh api \\\\\"repos/${REPO}/actions/runs?workflow_id=${WORKFLOW_ID}&status=success&created>=${prior_green_cutoff}\\\\\"`. The `$BRANCH` variable (parsed from `--branch` argument at line 169) is available but not appended as `&branch=${BRANCH}`. The DISPATCH-NOTE scope table entry for `--restore` mode states the safety check is \\'refuses forward-mode invocation against repo with no successful policy-check / scp/policy-check run on default branch in last 60 days\\'.\",\"impact\":\"The safety check could be satisfied by a green run on a stale PR branch that was later reverted, allowing `enable-required-check.sh` to proceed even if the adopter\\'s wrapper has never successfully run on their actual default branch. The foot-gun the safety check is meant to close (enabling the required-check before the wrapper is confirmed working on the branch it protects) remains partially open.\",\"mitigation\":\"Append `&branch=${BRANCH}` to the workflow runs API query: `gh api \\\\\"repos/${REPO}/actions/runs?workflow_id=${WORKFLOW_ID}&status=success&created>=${prior_green_cutoff}&branch=${BRANCH}\\\\\"`.\"},{\"id\":\"R1-COR-nit-001\",\"severity\":\"nit\",\"claim\":\"scripts/scaffold-downstream.sh line 163 calls `mkdir -p \\\\\"${OUTPUT_DIR}/.github\\\\\"` redundantly after line 150 already creates `${OUTPUT_DIR}/.github/workflows` via `mkdir -p \\\\\"$WORKFLOWS_DIR\\\\\"`, which implicitly creates the `.github` intermediate directory.\",\"evidence\":\"scripts/scaffold-downstream.sh line 149: `WORKFLOWS_DIR=\\\\\"${OUTPUT_DIR}/.github/workflows\\\\\"`. Line 150: `mkdir -p \\\\\"$WORKFLOWS_DIR\\\\\"` — creates `.github` as an intermediate directory. Line 163: `mkdir -p \\\\\"${OUTPUT_DIR}/.github\\\\\"` — no-op since `.github` already exists.\",\"impact\":\"No functional impact; `mkdir -p` on an existing directory is idempotent. Cosmetic redundancy.\",\"mitigation\":\"Remove line 163 (`mkdir -p \\\\\"${OUTPUT_DIR}/.github\\\\\"`).\"},{\"id\":\"R1-COR-nit-002\",\"severity\":\"nit\",\"claim\":\"check-invocation-log-entry.sh constructs slug-specific regex patterns (`TF-024X-renovate-{re.escape(slug)}...`) rather than embedding the plan-doc §2 invariant 2 literal regex byte-for-byte. While the deviation is deliberately stricter (prevents cross-adopter contamination), it creates silent drift risk if the plan-doc regex evolves.\",\"evidence\":\"scripts/check-invocation-log-entry.sh lines 192-194: `pattern = rf\\'TF-024X-renovate-{re.escape(slug)}(?:\\\\\\\\*\\\\\\\\*)? \\\\\\\\((open|pending|in-progress|closed)\\\\\\\\): \\\\\\\\S.{{19,}}\\'`. Plan-doc §2 invariant 2 literal: `TF-024X-(renovate|conflict)-[a-z0-9]+(?:-[a-z0-9]+)+(?:\\\\\\\\*\\\\\\\\*)? \\\\\\\\((open|pending|in-progress|closed)\\\\\\\\): \\\\\\\\S.{19,}`. The `(renovate|conflict)-[a-z0-9]+(?:-[a-z0-9]+)+` segment is replaced by the literal slug, meaning the status-enum set and description-length requirement are the only parts shared verbatim.\",\"impact\":\"If the plan-doc regex is updated (e.g., adding a new status value like `in-review` to the enum, or adjusting the slug character set), the script must be manually updated in parallel. The relationship between plan-doc spec and implementation is undocumented inline.\",\"mitigation\":\"Add an inline comment citing the plan-doc §2 invariant 2 generic regex and explaining the deliberate slug-specific substitution: `# Plan-doc §2 invariant 2 generic regex: TF-024X-(renovate|conflict)-[a-z0-9]+(?:-[a-z0-9]+)+... # Slug-specific pattern used here to prevent cross-adopter contamination.`\"}]}', 'stop_reason': 'end_turn', 'session_id': '64c6b14a-d228-4975-abf5-1d70263e8945', 'total_cost_usd': 1.5239660500000003, 'usage': {'input_tokens': 12, 'cache_creation_input_tokens': 136943, 'cache_read_input_tokens': 782426, 'output_tokens': 51546, 'server_tool_use': {'web_search_requests': 0, 'web_fetch_requests': 0}, 'service_tier': 'standard', 'cache_creation': {'ephemeral_1h_input_tokens': 136943, 'ephemeral_5m_input_tokens': 0}, 'inference_geo': '', 'iterations': [{'input_tokens': 1, 'output_tokens': 9191, 'cache_read_input_tokens': 153374, 'cache_creation_input_tokens': 490, 'cache_creation': {'ephemeral_5m_input_tokens': 0, 'ephemeral_1h_input_tokens': 490}, 'type': 'message'}], 'speed': 'standard'}, 'modelUsage': {'claude-haiku-4-5-20251001': {'inputTokens': 2356, 'outputTokens': 24, 'cacheReadInputTokens': 0, 'cacheCreationInputTokens': 0, 'webSearchRequests': 0, 'costUSD': 0.002476, 'contextWindow': 200000, 'maxOutputTokens': 32000}, 'claude-sonnet-4-6': {'inputTokens': 12, 'outputTokens': 51546, 'cacheReadInputTokens': 782426, 'cacheCreationInputTokens': 136943, 'webSearchRequests': 0, 'costUSD': 1.5214900500000001, 'contextWindow': 200000, 'maxOutputTokens': 32000}}, 'permission_denials': [], 'terminal_reason': 'completed', 'fast_mode_state': 'off', 'uuid': '841aa866-5c0f-4951-b3fd-97fd00b8d36a'}\nFILE docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/024B/r1-safety_bypass.json\ndict_keys(['type', 'subtype', 'is_error', 'api_error_status', 'duration_ms', 'duration_api_ms', 'num_turns', 'result', 'stop_reason', 'session_id', 'total_cost_usd', 'usage', 'modelUsage', 'permission_denials', 'terminal_reason', 'fast_mode_state', 'uuid'])\n{'type': 'result', 'subtype': 'success', 'is_error': False, 'api_error_status': None, 'duration_ms': 1423201, 'duration_api_ms': 1423584, 'num_turns': 15, 'result': '```json\\n{\\n  \"reviewer\": \"r2-safety_bypass\",\\n  \"lens\": \"safety_bypass\",\\n  \"slice\": \"WP-SCP-024-024B\",\\n  \"verdict\": \"CHANGES_REQUESTED\",\\n  \"summary\": \"No CRIT. Three MAJ, four MIN, three nit. Fail-closed default, blocked-on-adopter-conflict negative assertion, and CI=true bootstrap-guard ordering are all correctly implemented and verified by tests. Primary bypass surfaces: --restore applies degraded posture without confirmation guard; --restore emits no audit record on PUT failure (unlike forward-mode APPLY_FAIL trap); parse_entry_repo validates only the last diff entry, allowing a phantom second adopter entry in one PR to go unverified.\",\\n  \"findings\": [\\n    {\\n      \"id\": \"SAFE-001\",\\n      \"severity\": \"MAJ\",\\n      \"claim\": \"--restore mode applies a pre-state JSON with enforce_admins.enabled=false or empty required_status_checks.contexts without any confirmation guard, bypassing the forward-mode\\'s --no-enforce-admins acknowledgement requirement and 5-second pause.\",\\n      \"evidence\": \"enable-required-check.sh lines 378-381: the restore PUT fires unconditionally after structural JSON validation. Shape validator (lines 313-350) confirms enforce_admins.enabled is a bool but does NOT reject false. Forward-mode guard at lines 213-227 requires --i-understand-this-bypasses-the-gate AND a 5-second stderr pause before any PUT that sets enforce_admins=false. The --restore incompatibility guard (line 186) blocks forward-mode flags from being combined with --restore, but does not prevent the restored JSON itself from carrying enforce_admins.enabled:false or an empty contexts array.\",\\n      \"impact\": \"An operator who supplies a wrong or crafted pre-state.json silently downgrades an adopter repo\\'s branch protection (disables admin enforcement, clears required checks) with no friction. The invocation log block is emitted to stdout for paste, but pasting is operator-driven and skippable. The forward-mode\\'s explicit 5-second pause and ack-flag exist precisely to prevent this accidental downgrade; restore mode has no equivalent.\",\\n      \"mitigation\": \"After extracting restore_before_json, inspect enforce_admins.enabled; if false, emit a prominent stderr warning and require a new explicit flag (e.g. --i-understand-restore-removes-admin-enforcement) before proceeding. Similarly warn if required_status_checks.contexts will be empty. This mirrors lines 213-227 and closes the posture-degradation surface.\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \"id\": \"SAFE-002\",\\n      \"severity\": \"MAJ\",\\n      \"claim\": \"A failed --restore PUT (network error, 403, partial API failure) exits via set -e before the log emission block at line 391, leaving zero audit record of the attempted restore. Forward mode explicitly handles APPLY_FAIL via a trap that continues to log emission regardless of PUT outcome.\",\\n      \"evidence\": \"enable-required-check.sh lines 379-391: `gh api -X PUT ... >/dev/null` has no APPLY_FAIL=0 trap; `set -euo pipefail` at line 59 causes immediate exit on non-zero. The `cat <<EOF` log block at line 391 is never reached on PUT failure. Contrast forward-mode lines 529-551: `APPLY_FAIL=0` trap catches PUT failure, sets APPLY_FAIL=1, continues execution through log emission so a partial-state failure still produces an auditable log entry. This pattern was explicitly filed as closing 020G R2 safety SAF-R2-001.\",\\n      \"impact\": \"A restore that partially applies (e.g. PUT succeeds for status-checks but the session drops before verification at line 383) or fails entirely leaves the branch protection in an unknown state with no log record. The operator cannot distinguish success from failure without querying the GitHub API manually. The 020G R2 fix closed the identical gap for forward mode; restore mode was not extended with the same protection.\",\\n      \"mitigation\": \"Wrap the restore PUT in an APPLY_FAIL=0 guard identical to the forward-mode pattern (lines 529-551): set APPLY_FAIL=0 before the PUT, `|| APPLY_FAIL=1` on the PUT, capture restore_after_json regardless of APPLY_FAIL, continue to the log emission block in all cases, then exit 1 at the end if APPLY_FAIL=1.\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \"id\": \"SAFE-003\",\\n      \"severity\": \"MAJ\",\\n      \"claim\": \"parse_entry_repo (check-invocation-log-entry.sh lines 155-166) assigns last_repo by overwriting on each match, returning only the final +### TIMESTAMP — REPO@BRANCH line in the PR diff. A PR that adds two log entries (phantom entry for adopter Y first, correct entry for adopter X last) passes CI for X while the Y entry goes unverified.\",\\n      \"evidence\": \"check-invocation-log-entry.sh lines 158-164: `for line in patch: if line.startswith(\\\\\"+### \\\\\"): ... last_repo = match.group(1)` — each match overwrites. If a PR adds two invocation-log entries, only the last is checked against DISPATCH-NOTE target. Tests (test_check_invocation_log_entry.sh) use single-entry diffs; no test covers a two-entry diff where the second entry matches the target. The fake-gh case16 patch (lines 484-505) contains only one branch-protection-log entry.\",\\n      \"impact\": \"A cascade-slice PR can add a legitimate log entry for the declared adopter (last, passes check) and a phantom entry for a different adopter (first, unchecked). The phantom entry satisfies future per-adopter CI checks for the second adopter without a genuine invocation or cascade-status declaration. This bypasses invariant 2\\'s per-adopter discipline at the log-entry level.\",\\n      \"mitigation\": \"After iterating the patch, assert that exactly one +### match was found (count == 1); if count != 1 emit an error and exit 1. This converts the last-wins logic to an exactly-one assertion, closing the multi-entry bypass.\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \"id\": \"SAFE-004\",\\n      \"severity\": \"MIN\",\\n      \"claim\": \"scaffold-downstream.sh system-path deny list (lines 131-135) blocks /var exactly but not /var/* and blocks /tmp/* but not /tmp exactly, allowing --output-dir /var/log or --output-dir /tmp to pass unblocked.\",\\n      \"evidence\": \"scaffold-downstream.sh lines 131-135: case pattern `/etc|/etc/*|/var|/usr|/usr/*|/sys|/sys/*|/proc|/proc/*|/tmp/*`. The pattern /var matches the exact string /var but shell glob /var/* requires at least one character after the slash — /var/log does not match /var, and /var/* in a case statement matches /var/anything-with-chars. /tmp similarly is absent (only /tmp/* appears). test_scaffold_downstream.sh line 142 creates tmp_deny_dir via `mktemp -d /tmp/scaffold-deny.XXXXXX` which correctly hits /tmp/* — but /tmp itself and /var/log are not tested.\",\\n      \"impact\": \"An operator invoking `scaffold-downstream.sh --output-dir /var/log` writes policy-check-wrapper.yml, CODEOWNERS-snippet.txt, CASCADE-PR-BODY.md, and MANIFEST.json to /var/log without any warning. Similarly --output-dir /tmp writes to the root of the shared temp directory. No credential exposure; system path contamination and potential permission errors.\",\\n      \"mitigation\": \"Add /var/* and /tmp to the case pattern. Add /run/* and /dev/* for defence-in-depth. The full corrected pattern: `/etc|/etc/*|/var|/var/*|/usr|/usr/*|/sys|/sys/*|/proc|/proc/*|/tmp|/tmp/*|/run|/run/*|/dev|/dev/*`.\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \"id\": \"SAFE-005\",\\n      \"severity\": \"MIN\",\\n      \"claim\": \"The --restore mode log emission heredoc (enable-required-check.sh lines 391-422) interpolates restore_repo and restore_branch without sanitizing for backtick, CR, or LF characters, unlike the forward-mode\\'s REQUIRED_CONTEXT validation at lines 280-284.\",\\n      \"evidence\": \"enable-required-check.sh lines 280-284: forward mode rejects REQUIRED_CONTEXT containing backtick, CR, or LF with `case \\\\\"$REQUIRED_CONTEXT\\\\\" in *\\'\\\\\\\\`\\'*|...) ... exit 2`. Lines 391-422 restore-mode heredoc uses `${restore_repo}` and `${restore_branch}` directly without analogous sanitization. The Python JSON validator (lines 313-350) only checks that repo/branch are non-empty strings; it does not reject control characters or backticks.\",\\n      \"impact\": \"A crafted pre-state.json with `\\\\\"branch\\\\\": \\\\\"main\\\\\\\\`evil\\\\\\\\`\\\\\"` passes shape validation, passes path-traversal checks (no slash/dot sequences), and then the heredoc emits the backtick literally into the operator\\'s pasteable log block, corrupting the markdown. No code execution occurs (bash does not re-evaluate command substitutions within expanded variables in heredocs), but the pasted log entry in branch-protection-log.md is malformed, degrading audit-trail integrity.\",\\n      \"mitigation\": \"After extracting restore_repo and restore_branch via sed at lines 355-357, add a case guard analogous to lines 280-284: reject any value containing backtick, CR, or LF with exit 2 and an explanatory message.\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \"id\": \"SAFE-006\",\\n      \"severity\": \"MIN\",\\n      \"claim\": \"tests/scaffolder/test_scaffold_downstream.sh creates tmp_deny_dir via `mktemp -d /tmp/scaffold-deny.XXXXXX` (line 142) directly under /tmp, outside $TMPDIR. If the script exits early (test failure before line 150\\'s rm -rf), the trap cleanup only removes $TMPDIR and the fixture leaks.\",\\n      \"evidence\": \"test_scaffold_downstream.sh lines 9-12: `TMPDIR=$(mktemp -d); cleanup() { rm -rf \\\\\"$TMPDIR\\\\\"; }; trap cleanup EXIT`. Lines 142-150: `tmp_deny_dir=$(mktemp -d /tmp/scaffold-deny.XXXXXX); ... rm -rf \\\\\"$tmp_deny_dir\\\\\"`. The cleanup trap has no reference to tmp_deny_dir. If run_expect_exit at line 143 fails (unexpected exit code → `exit 1` inside run_expect_exit), the trap fires but only cleans $TMPDIR.\",\\n      \"impact\": \"Orphaned /tmp/scaffold-deny.* directories accumulate on repeated test failures. No credential or data leak. Minor test hygiene defect.\",\\n      \"mitigation\": \"Change line 142 to `mktemp -d \\\\\"${TMPDIR}/deny.XXXXXX\\\\\"` so the existing trap cleanup covers it and the manual rm -rf at line 150 becomes redundant.\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \"id\": \"SAFE-007\",\\n      \"severity\": \"MIN\",\\n      \"claim\": \"ADOPT-001 §12.8 Gate 3 depends on Gate 2 completion (wrapper SHA pinned to a release-tag SHA via `gh api .../git/ref/tags/<tag>`) but the dependency is documentation-only. enable-required-check.sh does not verify the live wrapper SHA pin before re-enabling the required check, so an operator can reach Gate 3 without Gate 2.\",\\n      \"evidence\": \"ADOPT-001 §12.8 Gate 3 step 3.0: `Confirm the SHA-pin matches the release-tag SHA captured in Gate 2 ... If the SHA-pin is NOT the release-tag SHA, return to Gate 2 - do NOT re-enable.` This is a documentation instruction. enable-required-check.sh forward mode (lines 248-270) only checks for prior green-CI runs; it does not fetch or inspect the adopter\\'s .github/workflows/policy-check-wrapper.yml SHA pin. Plan-doc invariant 7 and §12.8 rationale paragraph defend the 3-gate structure, but Gate 2→Gate 3 ordering has no code-level enforcement.\",\\n      \"impact\": \"An operator under pressure can execute Gate 1 (--restore), skip Gate 2 (wrapper re-pin), and immediately run Gate 3 (re-enable), re-arming the required check with the adopter\\'s wrapper still pinned to the defective SCP SHA. The very defect that triggered break-glass is perpetuated.\",\\n      \"mitigation\": \"Add an optional `--expected-wrapper-sha <sha>` flag to enable-required-check.sh forward mode. When supplied, the script fetches `gh api repos/${REPO}/contents/.github/workflows/policy-check-wrapper.yml` and verifies the pinned SHA matches before executing the PUT. A mismatch exits 2 with a diagnostic message. Document this flag in §12.8 Gate 3 step 3.1 as the machine-enforced Gate 2 verification.\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \"id\": \"SAFE-008\",\\n      \"severity\": \"nit\",\\n      \"claim\": \"D-044 row in DECISIONS.md describes the --restore before-state validation as checking \\'a legitimate GitHub branch-protection payload.\\' The word \\'legitimate\\' conflates structural validity (what the code enforces) with provenance (JSON genuinely captured from a prior invocation log entry, which is NOT verified). A future operator may treat passing validation as audit-provenance assurance.\",\\n      \"evidence\": \"docs/DECISIONS.md D-044 row: \\'validates that captured before-state as a legitimate GitHub branch-protection payload (required_status_checks object, enforce_admins.enabled boolean, required_pull_request_reviews object-or-null, restrictions object-or-null).\\' The enable-required-check.sh Python validator at lines 313-350 performs shape checks only; no hash, no log-entry cross-reference, no signature. ADOPT-001 §12.8 Gate 1 correctly states \\'The pre-state JSON lives at the bottom of every invocation log block ... copy it to a local file first before invoking\\' — but that instruction is in the procedure doc, not enforced by the script.\",\\n      \"impact\": \"Trust-model misstatement: operators reading D-044 may believe the script verifies the JSON is genuinely from a prior logged invocation and skip manual verification of the JSON content before invoking --restore with it.\",\\n      \"mitigation\": \"Change \\'legitimate\\' to \\'structurally valid\\' in the D-044 row and add a parenthetical: \\'(provenance — that the JSON genuinely originates from a prior branch-protection-log.md entry — is operator-verified, not script-verified)\\'.\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \"id\": \"SAFE-009\",\\n      \"severity\": \"nit\",\\n      \"claim\": \"The check-invocation-log-entry.sh test harness has no explicit negative case for a cascade-status value containing a space (e.g., cascade-status: not applicable without backtick-wrapping). The DISPATCH-NOTE correctly avoids this via backtick wrapping, but the fail-closed behaviour for the space-containing variant is untested.\",\\n      \"evidence\": \"tests/check_invocation_log/test_check_invocation_log_entry.sh cases 12-13 test absent cascade-status (0 matches) and unrecognised single-token value (FAIL-CLOSED branch). No case tests a space-containing value like `cascade-status: not applicable`. The regex `r\\'^cascade-status:\\\\\\\\s+([a-z-]+)\\\\\\\\s*$\\'` correctly returns 0 matches for this input (space terminates [a-z-]+ capture before end-of-line), but this is not validated in the harness. DISPATCH-NOTE.md line 9 uses backtick wrapping (`` `cascade-status:` not applicable ``) which correctly avoids the regex entirely.\",\\n      \"impact\": \"Test coverage gap on the fail-closed default for space-containing values. Risk is low because the DISPATCH-NOTE format is human-authored and the backtick convention is documented, but the 024A R7 CRIT lesson (\\'always test worked examples against the new regex\\') applies here.\",\\n      \"mitigation\": \"Add a test case (e.g. case 16) with DISPATCH-NOTE content `cascade-status: not applicable` (un-backticked) and assert exit 1 with \\'found 0: []\\' in stderr.\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \"id\": \"SAFE-010\",\\n      \"severity\": \"nit\",\\n      \"claim\": \"The meaningful-content format spec (\\\\\\\\S.{19,}) accepts description strings consisting entirely of non-whitespace punctuation (e.g., 20 hyphens: \\'--------------------\\'). The worked-example negative cases (test harness cases 5-8) test only empty, whitespace-only, and short descriptions — not punctuation-only strings of sufficient length.\",\\n      \"evidence\": \"check-invocation-log-entry.sh lines 192-193: `pattern = rf\\'TF-024X-renovate-{re.escape(slug)}(?:\\\\\\\\*\\\\\\\\*)? \\\\\\\\((open|pending|in-progress|closed)\\\\\\\\): \\\\\\\\S.{{19,}}\\'`. \\\\\\\\S matches any non-whitespace including `-`, `!`, `*`. A description `--------------------` (20 hyphens) satisfies \\\\\\\\S (first hyphen) and .{19,} (19 more). Test case 7 checks all-whitespace (correctly fails); no case checks punctuation-only. DISPATCH-NOTE worked examples (lines 62-70) similarly test only whitespace and length failure modes.\",\\n      \"impact\": \"A TF row like `- **TF-024X-renovate-jrnb2024-pim** (open): --------------------` passes CI. Semantic emptiness is not detectable via regex; human review is the remaining control. This is an acknowledged design limitation (the spec is explicitly a syntactic floor) but the test suite does not document this boundary by including a passing punctuation-only case.\",\\n      \"mitigation\": \"No code change required — the regex is correctly scoped as a syntactic guard. Add a comment in the test harness and in DISPATCH-NOTE §Cascade-status spec recap explicitly noting that punctuation-only descriptions of ≥20 chars satisfy the regex and that semantic meaningfulness is enforced by human review of the DISPATCH-NOTE, not by CI.\"\\n    }\\n  ]\\n}\\n```', 'stop_reason': 'end_turn', 'session_id': 'a4da6b86-b18a-47f8-82f6-cd4131f486e5', 'total_cost_usd': 1.7587870499999998, 'usage': {'input_tokens': 15, 'cache_creation_input_tokens': 137489, 'cache_read_input_tokens': 642351, 'output_tokens': 69868, 'server_tool_use': {'web_search_requests': 0, 'web_fetch_requests': 0}, 'service_tier': 'standard', 'cache_creation': {'ephemeral_1h_input_tokens': 137489, 'ephemeral_5m_input_tokens': 0}, 'inference_geo': '', 'iterations': [{'input_tokens': 3, 'output_tokens': 4552, 'cache_read_input_tokens': 112871, 'cache_creation_input_tokens': 31999, 'cache_creation': {'ephemeral_5m_input_tokens': 0, 'ephemeral_1h_input_tokens': 31999}, 'type': 'message'}], 'speed': 'standard'}, 'modelUsage': {'claude-haiku-4-5-20251001': {'inputTokens': 2323, 'outputTokens': 22, 'cacheReadInputTokens': 0, 'cacheCreationInputTokens': 0, 'webSearchRequests': 0, 'costUSD': 0.002433, 'contextWindow': 200000, 'maxOutputTokens': 32000}, 'claude-sonnet-4-6': {'inputTokens': 15, 'outputTokens': 69868, 'cacheReadInputTokens': 642351, 'cacheCreationInputTokens': 137489, 'webSearchRequests': 0, 'costUSD': 1.7563540499999999, 'contextWindow': 200000, 'maxOutputTokens': 32000}}, 'permission_denials': [], 'terminal_reason': 'completed', 'fast_mode_state': 'off', 'uuid': '71df8465-e738-4640-8a0f-ed2b7b1186c9'}\nFILE docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/024B/r1-completeness_governance.json\ndict_keys(['type', 'subtype', 'is_error', 'api_error_status', 'duration_ms', 'duration_api_ms', 'num_turns', 'result', 'stop_reason', 'session_id', 'total_cost_usd', 'usage', 'modelUsage', 'permission_denials', 'terminal_reason', 'fast_mode_state', 'uuid'])\n{'type': 'result', 'subtype': 'success', 'is_error': False, 'api_error_status': None, 'duration_ms': 671127, 'duration_api_ms': 671998, 'num_turns': 19, 'result': 'Now I have all the information needed. Producing the structured findings report.\\n\\n{\"review_id\":\"r1-completeness_governance\",\"lens\":\"completeness_governance\",\"slice_id\":\"WP-SCP-024/024B\",\"branch\":\"feature/wp-scp-024-024b-scaffolder\",\"reviewed_at\":\"2026-05-09\",\"verdict\":\"changes_requested\",\"crit_count\":0,\"maj_count\":3,\"min_count\":4,\"nit_count\":1,\"findings\":[{\"id\":\"MAJ-AC-001\",\"severity\":\"MAJ\",\"claim\":\"AC 2 (--restore round-trip on real throw-away repo) is unchecked and the evidence file is absent; slice is not mergeable in this state.\",\"evidence\":\"DISPATCH-NOTE §Acceptance criteria item 2 is explicitly unchecked: \\'- [ ] --restore round-trip via captured before-state restores branch-protection state byte-for-byte on a real throw-away test repo (not a dry-run mock — closes 024A R1 MAJ-SAFE-003).\\' STATUS.md 024B chain row states \\'PENDING — operator-interactive demo at step 7 of dispatch plan; evidence path docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/024B/restore-roundtrip-evidence.md to be committed post-demo, pre-merge, per plan-doc §6 acceptance criterion.\\' Glob for restore-roundtrip-evidence.md returns no results — file is absent from the branch. DISPATCH-NOTE §Dispatch plan step 7 is the operator-interactive demo; DISPATCH-NOTE §Sequencing table shows Phase 6 (--restore demo) and Phase 7 (self-merge) are both future phases.\",\"impact\":\"Plan-doc invariant 7 SLO (<30 min single-operator rollback) remains explicitly aspirational per plan-doc §10 Q3: \\'closed only when 024B\\'s acceptance criterion in §6 is met — i.e. a real-repo round-trip on a throw-away test repo.\\' 024C MUST NOT open without demonstrated round-trip per plan-doc §6 + DISPATCH-NOTE §Cohort cascade gate. The slice is not mergeable and the 024C gate is not proven.\",\"mitigation\":\"Complete operator-interactive demo (dispatch plan step 7): create throw-away test repo (e.g. jrnb2024/scp-024b-restore-test), capture before-state JSON, run enable-required-check.sh forward mode, run --restore mode, verify branch-protection API response matches captured before-state byte-for-byte, commit evidence to docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/024B/restore-roundtrip-evidence.md before merge. No descoping — this is a hard AC per plan-doc §6.\"},{\"id\":\"MAJ-TEST-001\",\"severity\":\"MAJ\",\"claim\":\"Test harness missing a positive onboarded-operator-bump case for Example 1 (**-bold STATUS.md format), directly violating the 024A R7 CRIT lesson mandate to test all worked examples against the new regex.\",\"evidence\":\"DISPATCH-NOTE §Scope table for check-invocation-log-entry.sh tests: \\'Each of the 4 CI behaviours has at least one positive + one negative case; regex anchors tested with worked examples (per 024A R7 CRIT lesson — always test worked examples against the new regex).\\' Plan-doc §2 invariant 2 and DISPATCH-NOTE §Cascade-status spec recap both show two positive worked examples: Example 1 \\'- **TF-024X-renovate-jrnb2024-pim** (open): Renovate disabled on PIM; operator-bumped @abc123 at 024C; track until adopter enables Renovate cohort.\\' (STATUS.md bullet with **-bold closing marker) and Example 2 \\'See TF-024X-conflict-jrnb2024-pim (pending): adopter has prior policy-check workflow; awaiting rename PR.\\' (prose embedding without **). test_check_invocation_log_entry.sh case 4 (only positive onboarded-operator-bump case) sets STATUS.md to \\'See TF-024X-renovate-jrnb2024-pim (open): Renovate disabled on PIM; operator-bumped @abc123 at 024C; track until adopter enables Renovate cohort.\\' — this is Example 2 format (no ** closing-bold). init_case_repo sets up default STATUS.md with \\'- **TF-024X-renovate-jrnb2024-base** (open): placeholder base row for repo setup.\\' but slug \\'base\\' never matches the target \\'pim\\' in any test case. No test case uses \\'- **TF-024X-renovate-jrnb2024-pim** (open): ...\\' in STATUS.md for the onboarded-operator-bump positive path.\",\"impact\":\"The (?:\\\\\\\\*\\\\\\\\*)? optional group in the onboarded-operator-bump regex (added specifically to close 024A R7 CRIT-001 — the single most expensive fix in the 024A review chain at fix-round-7) is not exercised in any positive onboarded-operator-bump case. A future regression that drops the optional group would not be caught. This contradicts the direct mandate of the 024A R7 CRIT lesson that this fix-round addressed.\",\"mitigation\":\"Add a positive test case (e.g. case 4b) where STATUS.md contains \\'- **TF-024X-renovate-jrnb2024-pim** (open): Renovate disabled on PIM; operator-bumped @abc123 at 024C; track until adopter enables Renovate cohort.\\' (Example 1 format — leading dash, **-bold slug, closing **). Run: run_case \\\\\"$repo_dir\\\\\" 0 \\'OK: cascade-status=onboarded-operator-bump; 3 checks passed\\' \\'\\'. This is additive — no existing tests need changing. One new case closes the coverage gap.\"},{\"id\":\"MAJ-TF-001\",\"severity\":\"MAJ\",\"claim\":\"FUP-ACC-INSTALL-TARGET-REPO-001 gates 024C entry but has no formal STATUS.md tracked-forward row; the canonical resume surface is missing this gate condition.\",\"evidence\":\"STATUS.md has no \\'Tracked-forward items from 024B\\' section. FUP-ACC-INSTALL-TARGET-REPO-001 appears only in prose across four locations: STATUS.md 024B chain row (\\'cohort cascade 024C-F gated on FUP-ACC-INSTALL-TARGET-REPO-001 closure\\'), DISPATCH-NOTE §Protocol deviation (\\'filed FUP-ACC-INSTALL-TARGET-REPO-001 on ACC for cohort cascade\\'), DISPATCH-NOTE §Cohort cascade gate (\\'024C entry is conditional on FUP-ACC-INSTALL-TARGET-REPO-001 closure\\'), and plan-doc §6 amendment (\\'Cohort dispatcher precondition (added 2026-05-09)\\'). All prior slice-gating tracked-forward items have formal STATUS.md rows — compare TF-023E-001 and TF-023E-002 in \\'Tracked-forward items from WP-SCP-023 023A + 023B + 023C\\', each with a full description and status in parenthetical.\",\"impact\":\"An operator resuming work post-024B merge in a new session will check STATUS.md tracked-forward items for open gates before opening 024C — and will find none, because FUP-ACC-INSTALL-TARGET-REPO-001 is buried in the 024B chain row prose and in the DISPATCH-NOTE. The governance audit trail for the 024C gate condition is incomplete at the canonical tracking surface (STATUS.md TF section). Risk: 024C opens prematurely before FUP-ACC-INSTALL-TARGET-REPO-001 closes on the ACC side.\",\"mitigation\":\"Add a \\'Tracked-forward items from 024B\\' section to STATUS.md with a row for FUP-ACC-INSTALL-TARGET-REPO-001. Suggested row (format mirrors TF-023E-001 pattern): \\'- **FUP-ACC-INSTALL-TARGET-REPO-001** (open): ACC-side per-repo dispatcher install; install_acc_hook.sh needs --target-repo flag + cross-repo kernel-config validation; 024C MUST NOT open until closed; ACC work item filed 2026-05-09; blocks full four-tier dispatch for all cohort cascade slices 024C-F.\\' The row need not match the cascade-status TF regex (that regex is for cascade-status-specific TFs) but should follow the broader SCP TF tracking convention.\"},{\"id\":\"MIN-SCHEMA-001\",\"severity\":\"MIN\",\"claim\":\"Scaffolder test uses string-match assertions rather than formal YAML schema validation; AC 1 language (\\'schema-validates against the canonical wrapper shape from ADOPT-001 §12\\') implies a validation step beyond grep.\",\"evidence\":\"AC 1: \\'Scaffolder emits a known-fixture wrapper that schema-validates against the canonical wrapper shape from ADOPT-001 §12.\\' tests/scaffolder/test_scaffold_downstream.sh validates the emitted wrapper via three grep -Fq checks: \\'branches: [main]\\', the SCP SHA string, and \\'scorecard-emit: false\\'. It also validates MANIFEST.json structure via a Python validator that checks required keys and SHA256 round-trips. No canonical YAML/JSON schema file exists for the wrapper shape, and no schema validation step (e.g. via python3 -c \\'import yaml, jsonschema; jsonschema.validate(yaml.safe_load(open(wrapper).read()), schema)\\') is invoked. The template (adopter-wrapper.yml.tmpl) is the canonical shape, but the test does not assert structural invariants (e.g. presence of \\'name:\\', \\'on.pull_request.branches\\', \\'permissions.contents\\', \\'jobs.policy-check.uses\\', \\'jobs.policy-check.with.scorecard-emit\\').\",\"impact\":\"If the template gains structural changes (e.g. an additional required YAML key or a renamed permissions field), the string-match tests would still pass while the emitted wrapper diverges from the documented canonical shape. The \\'schema-validates\\' AC language creates a false expectation of formal structural validation that isn\\'t provided. This is a documentation/AC drift risk rather than an immediate functional gap.\",\"mitigation\":\"Two acceptable paths: (a) Introduce a minimal YAML schema (JSON Schema draft-7 or Pydantic model) for the adopter wrapper and add a schema-validation step to the test asserting the emitted YAML passes structural validation — at minimum: \\'name\\' == \\'policy-check\\', \\'on.pull_request.branches\\' is a list of length 1, \\'permissions.contents\\' == \\'read\\', \\'jobs.policy-check.uses\\' matches the expected pattern, \\'jobs.policy-check.with.scorecard-emit\\' is a boolean; OR (b) Amend the AC language from \\'schema-validates against the canonical wrapper shape\\' to \\'verifies template substitutions match the canonical wrapper shape via string-assertion checks\\' to accurately reflect the test approach and add an exhaustive structural assertion list.\"},{\"id\":\"MIN-DOC-001\",\"severity\":\"MIN\",\"claim\":\"CASCADE-PR-BODY.md template uses additive language (\\'This PR adds...\\') but lacks an explicit invariant 3 statement that no existing adopter files are modified, leaving adopter reviewers without a concrete check to perform.\",\"evidence\":\"Plan-doc §2 invariant 3: \\'Cascade does NOT touch existing adopter content.\\' CASCADE-PR-BODY.md (emitted at scripts/scaffold-downstream.sh lines 169–184) explicitly surfaces: invariant 1 (consent surface, line 178), invariant 9 (bus-factor-1, line 173), invariant 4/D-036 (version-skew, line 181), invariant 5 (scorecard-emit opt-in, line 183). It does not include a statement equivalent to \\'Per cascade invariant 3: this PR is purely additive — no existing files in this repository are modified.\\' The additive posture is implied by \\'This PR adds the SCP wrapper...\\' (line 170) but not made explicit as a guarantee adopter reviewers can verify.\",\"impact\":\"Adopter maintainers reviewing the cascade PR see explicit invariant 1, 9, and 5 disclosures but no invariant 3 assertion. If a future cascade slice inadvertently includes a file modification (violating invariant 10), the PR body provides no explicit negative assertion that would prompt adopter reviewers to flag the deviation. This is a hardening gap rather than a current violation — the scaffolder is correctly additive-only.\",\"mitigation\":\"Add a sentence to the CASCADE-PR-BODY.md template in scaffold-downstream.sh: \\'Per cascade invariant 3: this PR is purely additive — no existing files in this repository are modified. If your diff view shows any modification to an existing file, please flag this to the cascade operator before merging.\\' This gives adopter reviewers a specific, actionable check and closes the implicit-vs-explicit gap.\"},{\"id\":\"MIN-DOC-002\",\"severity\":\"MIN\",\"claim\":\"DISPATCH-NOTE §Protocol deviation references specific ACC line numbers (codex_dispatch.py:594, _hook_integrity.py:142-143) that will become stale when FUP-ACC-INSTALL-TARGET-REPO-001 closes and ACC refactors those files.\",\"evidence\":\"DISPATCH-NOTE §Protocol deviation: \\'_verify_hook_or_exit(cwd) (codex_dispatch.py:594) calls verify_hook_integrity(repo_root) which looks for <repo_root>/acc.kernel.config (_hook_integrity.py:142-143).\\' FUP-ACC-INSTALL-TARGET-REPO-001 is the ACC work item specifically to fix install_acc_hook.sh and the dispatcher\\'s per-repo validation logic — i.e. exactly the files and functions whose line numbers are cited. Once FUP closes, ACC will have refactored these files, silently invalidating the line-number references cited as the root cause of the protocol deviation.\",\"impact\":\"Post-FUP-ACC-INSTALL-TARGET-REPO-001 closure, readers referencing the 024B DISPATCH-NOTE to understand the ACC integration history will find line numbers pointing to the wrong code. This degrades the forensic traceability value of the protocol deviation rationale, which is otherwise a thorough and accurate record.\",\"mitigation\":\"Replace specific line numbers with stable identifiers: cite the ACC commit SHA at time of writing plus function names only. E.g., replace \\'(codex_dispatch.py:594)\\' with \\'(codex_dispatch.py, function _verify_hook_or_exit, ACC at 2026-05-09)\\' and \\'(_hook_integrity.py:142-143)\\' with \\'(_hook_integrity.py, function verify_hook_integrity, ACC at 2026-05-09)\\'. Alternatively, add a parenthetical note: \\'(line numbers as of ACC 2026-05-09; may shift when FUP-ACC-INSTALL-TARGET-REPO-001 closes)\\'. Either approach preserves the forensic value without creating silent rot.\"},{\"id\":\"MIN-DOC-003\",\"severity\":\"MIN\",\"claim\":\"DISPATCH-NOTE status footer has not been updated past Phase 0 wording after implementation completed, causing a false \\'not yet started\\' impression for readers.\",\"evidence\":\"DISPATCH-NOTE final line: \\'**Status:** DRAFT v0.1 — awaiting kernel-hook install confirmation, then Codex T3 executor dispatch.\\' This was accurate at Phase 0 (DISPATCH-NOTE authoring). The implementation has been delivered: all code exists on the branch (scripts/scaffold-downstream.sh, templates/adopter-wrapper.yml.tmpl, enable-required-check.sh --restore, scripts/check-invocation-log-entry.sh, tests/scaffolder/test_scaffold_downstream.sh, tests/check_invocation_log/test_check_invocation_log_entry.sh, ADOPT-001 §12.8, D-044 in DECISIONS.md, STATUS.md update). Recent commits confirm: \\'WP-SCP-024 024B implementation\\' (7440f5c), \\'audit logs (deviated-dispatch compensating discipline)\\' (1a280ad), \\'fix-round-1\\' (c5d4922). Current phase is Phase 4 (R1 review) with Phase 6 (--restore demo) pending.\",\"mitigation\":\"Update DISPATCH-NOTE status to reflect current phase: \\'**Status:** v0.2 — implementation complete (Phase 2); compensating discipline logs committed (Phase 3); R1 underway (Phase 4). Pending before merge: R(N) fixpoint (Phase 5) + operator --restore real-repo demo + restore-roundtrip-evidence.md commit (Phase 6) + self-merge (Phase 7).\\' This makes the resume state legible for any operator picking up the slice post-R1.\"},{\"id\":\"nit-SEQNUM-001\",\"severity\":\"nit\",\"claim\":\"Sequencing table phase numbers (0-7) and Dispatch plan step numbers (1-8) use different zero-vs-one indexing, causing Phase 6 = Step 7 (--restore demo) — a cross-reference offset that can mislead readers.\",\"evidence\":\"Dispatch plan §Dispatch plan lists steps 1-8: (1) pre-dispatch scope check, (2) Codex executor, (3) post-dispatch scope check, (4) verify-commands manual run, (5) 3x parallel Sonnet R1, (6) fix-rounds, (7) --restore demo, (8) self-merge. Sequencing table lists phases 0-7: Phase 0 = DISPATCH-NOTE authoring (absent from dispatch plan\\'s step-1-start), Phase 3 = post-dispatch scope check + verify-commands (combines dispatch steps 3+4). This makes Sequencing table Phase 6 = --restore demo = Dispatch plan Step 7. A reader cross-referencing \\'Phase 6\\' to \\'Step 6\\' (fix-rounds) finds the wrong description.\",\"impact\":\"Minor readability confusion for reviewers or operators following both sections in parallel. Does not affect correctness of either section independently. Does not create a governance gap.\",\"mitigation\":\"Add a one-line note to the Sequencing table header: \\'Note: Phase 0 covers pre-dispatch DISPATCH-NOTE authoring; Phase 3 combines Dispatch plan steps 3+4 (post-dispatch scope check + verify-commands); phases map to Dispatch plan steps with a +1 offset from Phase 1 onward.\\' Alternatively, start the Sequencing table at Phase 1 (DISPATCH-NOTE authoring) so Phase 7 = Step 8 (self-merge) and the offset disappears.\"}],\"ac_mapping\":{\"ac1_scaffolder_schema_validates\":\"PARTIAL — template substitutions verified by string-match assertions (grep); no formal YAML schema validation against a canonical schema; AC language implies formal validation (MIN-SCHEMA-001)\",\"ac2_restore_roundtrip_real_repo\":\"UNSATISFIED — operator-interactive demo (dispatch plan step 7) pending; restore-roundtrip-evidence.md absent from branch (MAJ-AC-001); merge blocked\",\"ac3_check_invocation_log_4_behaviours\":\"SATISFIED — script correctly implements all four paths (fail-closed, onboarded, onboarded-operator-bump, blocked-on-adopter-conflict); 16 test cases cover all named scenarios; regex implementation matches plan-doc spec; **-bold format regex (?:\\\\\\\\*\\\\\\\\*)? implemented correctly but Example 1 positive case not explicitly tested (MAJ-TEST-001)\",\"ac4_d044_filed\":\"SATISFIED — D-044 entry present in docs/DECISIONS.md with correct content covering all four components (scaffolder + template + --restore + check-invocation-log-entry.sh + ADOPT-001 §12.8); assigned to 024B not to any other decision\",\"ac5_hard_predecessors_for_024c\":\"SATISFIED — DISPATCH-NOTE §Cohort cascade gate and plan-doc §6 amendment both name all three components as hard predecessors; FUP-ACC-INSTALL-TARGET-REPO-001 named as additional gate; lacks formal STATUS.md TF row (MAJ-TF-001)\",\"ac6_adopt001_break_glass_playbook\":\"SATISFIED — ADOPT-001 §12.8 written between §12.7.15 (line 1128) and §13 (line 1210); 3-gate playbook complete; Gate 1 (enable-required-check.sh --restore), Gate 2 (policy-check-wrapper.yml SHA-pin to known-good release tag), Gate 3 (re-enable); SLO <30 min named; Reference line includes D-044 + WP-SCP-024 invariant 7 + D-035 + ADOPT-001 §12.7; §12.7 Reference line gains D-044\"},\"invariant_checks\":{\"inv1_opt_in_consent\":\"SATISFIED — CASCADE-PR-BODY.md explicitly names invariant 1 consent surface; adopter wrapper PR is the audit record for the SHA-pin choice\",\"inv2_operator_invocation_ci_enforced\":\"SATISFIED — enable-required-check.sh refuses CI=true/GITHUB_ACTIONS=true (lines 63-67); check-invocation-log-entry.sh enforces all four CI behaviours; tests cover all paths\",\"inv3_no_existing_content_modified\":\"PARTIAL — scaffolder is purely additive (creates new files only); CASCADE-PR-BODY.md uses additive language but lacks explicit negative assertion for adopter reviewers (MIN-DOC-001)\",\"inv4_fla_independent\":\"SATISFIED — no FLA reference as precondition anywhere in 024B; DISPATCH-NOTE §FLA pilot safety findings reviewed explicitly confirms no FLA implications; plan-doc §6 amendment makes no FLA reference\",\"inv5_required_check_primary_scorecard_secondary\":\"SATISFIED — CASCADE-PR-BODY.md surfaces scorecard-emit as opt-in and deferred per TF-023E-002\",\"inv6_no_cross_repo_write_automation\":\"SATISFIED — scaffolder bootstrap-only; CI=true/GITHUB_ACTIONS=true refusal at lines 48-50; test cases 3 and 4 verify CI/GITHUB_ACTIONS refusal exits non-zero\",\"inv7_rollback_slo_measurable\":\"PARTIAL — SLO \\'<30 min from operator decision to restored state\\' documented in ADOPT-001 §12.8; --restore implementation correct; real-repo round-trip not yet demonstrated (MAJ-AC-001 pending)\",\"inv8_post_bake_not_in_scope\":\"SATISFIED — 024B makes no claim to satisfy invariant 8; correctly deferred to 024C+ as post-bake observation window\",\"inv9_bus_factor_acknowledged\":\"SATISFIED — CASCADE-PR-BODY.md explicitly discloses invariant 9 and D-031 with 2026-07-21 quarterly review named\",\"inv10_no_backwards_incompatible\":\"SATISFIED — scaffolder only creates new files (policy-check-wrapper.yml, CODEOWNERS-snippet.txt, CASCADE-PR-BODY.md, MANIFEST.json); no modification of existing files; test confirms output-dir contents are new\"},\"d_nnn_reservation_check\":\"SATISFIED — D-044 correctly assigned to 024B components; content matches reservation note in DECISIONS.md header (scaffolder operational contract + adopter-template versioning + invocation-log convention extension + --restore mode); D-045 and D-046 remain reserved and unassigned\",\"acc_notifications_dir_check\":\"SATISFIED — scaffolder does NOT establish ~/Projects/acc/docs/notifications/ dir; correctly deferred to 024C kickoff per DISPATCH-NOTE §Scope (out): \\'ACC notifications dir establishment deferred to 024C kickoff per plan-doc §5.5 row 2\\'\",\"versioning_check\":\"SATISFIED — scaffolder and check-invocation-log-entry.sh are internal tooling (not in VERSIONING.md public surface scope); enable-required-check.sh --restore and --i-understand-this-repo-has-no-prior-green-ci additions are additive (no breaking change); new flags are now registered in VERSIONING.md §Branch-protection helper breaking-change list; no version bump required for this slice\",\"tf_tracked_forward_check\":{\"fup_acc_install_target_repo_001\":\"DISPATCH-NOTE explicitly names as 024C entry precondition in §Cohort cascade gate and §Protocol deviation; plan-doc §6 amendment contains the text; STATUS.md lacks formal tracked-forward row (MAJ-TF-001)\",\"tf_024_restore_roundtrip_evidence\":\"Captured in DISPATCH-NOTE §Acceptance criteria (unchecked AC item 2) and §Scope table (evidence file path named); not registered as a named TF item — treated as a dispatch plan step (step 7) rather than a tracked-forward; design intent is correct; the unchecked AC is the merge gate\"},\"sequencing_table_status\":\"Phase 0 (DISPATCH-NOTE): complete. Phase 1 (pre-dispatch scope check): complete per commit 1a280ad (audit logs). Phase 2 (Codex executor): complete per commit 7440f5c. Phase 3 (post-dispatch + verify-commands): complete per commit 1a280ad. Phase 4 (R1 review): in progress (this review). Phase 5 (fix-rounds): pending. Phase 6 (--restore demo): pending (MAJ-AC-001). Phase 7 (self-merge): pending. No Sequencing table entry makes a false claim about completion state.\",\"summary\":\"Slice 024B delivers its core mandate — scaffolder, adopter wrapper template, --restore mode, check-invocation-log-entry.sh covering all four CI behaviours, ADOPT-001 §12.8 break-glass, D-044, and STATUS.md tracking — and the implementation is substantively correct. Three MAJ findings require closure before merge: (1) the --restore real-repo round-trip AC is explicitly unchecked and the evidence file absent (operator-interactive demo at dispatch plan step 7 not yet performed); (2) the test harness is missing a positive onboarded-operator-bump case for the **-bold STATUS.md format, violating the explicit 024A R7 CRIT lesson mandate; (3) FUP-ACC-INSTALL-TARGET-REPO-001 lacks a formal STATUS.md tracked-forward row despite gating 024C entry. Four MIN items cover: schema validation gap in AC language vs test approach; missing explicit invariant 3 disclosure in CASCADE-PR-BODY.md; ACC line-number documentation rot risk in the protocol deviation section; and DISPATCH-NOTE status footer not updated past Phase 0. One nit on sequencing table / dispatch plan numbering offset. No CRIT findings; no plan-doc invariant violations; no descoping detected; all D-NNN reservations honored; VERSIONING.md correctly scoped.\"}', 'stop_reason': 'end_turn', 'session_id': '2ec5e6c6-ac2f-470d-848e-a7f5d6cbf355', 'total_cost_usd': 1.2986981499999999, 'usage': {'input_tokens': 11, 'cache_creation_input_tokens': 141319, 'cache_read_input_tokens': 776103, 'output_tokens': 35546, 'server_tool_use': {'web_search_requests': 0, 'web_fetch_requests': 0}, 'service_tier': 'standard', 'cache_creation': {'ephemeral_1h_input_tokens': 141319, 'ephemeral_5m_input_tokens': 0}, 'inference_geo': '', 'iterations': [{'input_tokens': 1, 'output_tokens': 6670, 'cache_read_input_tokens': 147952, 'cache_creation_input_tokens': 748, 'cache_creation': {'ephemeral_5m_input_tokens': 0, 'ephemeral_1h_input_tokens': 748}, 'type': 'message'}], 'speed': 'standard'}, 'modelUsage': {'claude-haiku-4-5-20251001': {'inputTokens': 2578, 'outputTokens': 24, 'cacheReadInputTokens': 0, 'cacheCreationInputTokens': 0, 'webSearchRequests': 0, 'costUSD': 0.002698, 'contextWindow': 200000, 'maxOutputTokens': 32000}, 'claude-sonnet-4-6': {'inputTokens': 11, 'outputTokens': 35546, 'cacheReadInputTokens': 776103, 'cacheCreationInputTokens': 141319, 'webSearchRequests': 0, 'costUSD': 1.2960001499999998, 'contextWindow': 200000, 'maxOutputTokens': 32000}}, 'permission_denials': [], 'terminal_reason': 'completed', 'fast_mode_state': 'off', 'uuid': '6f4a130f-886b-4752-90b3-65b051d38bdc'}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
